@ttran2212 This is a truly foolish way of thinking. The reason foreign companies are leaving China and setting up factories in Vietnam is simply that Vietnam offers cheap labor. While this may contribute to Vietnam’s economic development, it cannot be considered the growth of Vietnamese domestic companies. Vietnam is not acquiring core technologies; it is merely providing low-cost labor for basic assembly operations.

As labor costs in Vietnam rise in the future, foreign companies will relocate their factories to other countries where labor is cheaper. When that happens, Vietnam will be left without core technologies of its own, with a workforce that knows little beyond basic assembly.

So let me ask you a question: Does Vietnam have any globally successful companies that possess core Fourth Industrial Revolution technologies? And please, don’t bring up VinFast. It is essentially a company that imports Chinese components and assembles them. In terms of industry competitiveness, it consistently operates at a loss and is nowhere near the top of its industry rankings.
